e:["$","section",null,{"className":"py-20 lg:py-24","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 lg:px-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"text-center","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-teal-600","children":"Frequently Asked Questions"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-3xl font-bold sm:text-4xl","children":"Allergies & Allergic Rhinitis FAQs"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-5 text-lg text-slate-600","children":"Answers to common questions about allergy symptoms, diagnosis, treatment and ENT evaluation."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-10","children":["$","$L27",null,{"faqItems":[{"question":"What is an allergy?","answer":"An allergy occurs when the immune system reacts to a normally harmless substance, known as an allergen. Common allergens include pollen, house dust mites, mould, animal dander, certain foods, insect stings and some medicines."},{"question":"What are the symptoms of allergic rhinitis?","answer":"Common symptoms include repeated sneezing, an itchy nose, runny or blocked nose, itchy or watery eyes and sometimes cough or an itchy throat. Symptoms often occur after exposure to a particular trigger."},{"question":"What causes nasal allergies?","answer":"Common triggers include pollen, house dust mites, mould and animal dander. Identifying individual triggers can help with long-term symptom management."},{"question":"How can I tell if my symptoms are caused by an allergy?","answer":"Symptoms such as repeated sneezing, nasal itching, clear runny discharge and itchy or watery eyes that repeatedly occur after exposure to a particular trigger may suggest an allergy. However, infections and non-allergic rhinitis can produce similar symptoms, so persistent or unclear symptoms should be evaluated by a doctor."},{"question":"Is allergic rhinitis the same as sinusitis?","answer":"No. Allergic rhinitis is caused by an allergic reaction, while sinusitis involves inflammation of the sinuses and may occur because of infection, inflammation or other factors. The two conditions can sometimes occur together."},{"question":"Can allergies cause a blocked nose?","answer":"Yes. Allergic inflammation can cause swelling of the nasal lining and lead to nasal blockage. Persistent blockage may also have other causes, so an ENT evaluation can be helpful when symptoms continue."},{"question":"Can allergies cause post-nasal drip?","answer":"Yes. Nasal inflammation can increase mucus production and cause mucus to drain towards the back of the throat. This may lead to frequent throat clearing, cough or throat irritation."},{"question":"What treatments are available for allergies?","answer":"Treatment depends on the type and severity of the allergy. Options may include trigger avoidance, saline nasal rinses, antihistamines and nasal corticosteroid sprays. Severe or difficult-to-control allergies may require specialist assessment and, in selected patients, allergy immunotherapy."},{"question":"When should I see an ENT specialist for allergies?","answer":"Consider an ENT consultation if symptoms are persistent, interfere with sleep or daily activities, cause significant nasal blockage, keep returning despite treatment, or if the diagnosis is uncertain."},{"question":"Can allergies affect sleep?","answer":"Yes. Persistent nasal congestion can make comfortable nasal breathing difficult and may disturb sleep. If significant snoring, mouth breathing or sleep-disordered breathing is also present, an ENT assessment may be appropriate."},{"question":"Are allergy tests necessary for everyone?","answer":"No. Testing is not required for every person with mild, typical allergy symptoms. When the trigger is unclear, symptoms are severe or treatment is not working adequately, a doctor may consider allergy testing such as skin-prick or blood testing."},{"question":"Can allergies be permanently cured?","answer":"Some allergies can be effectively controlled by avoiding triggers and using appropriate treatment. For selected allergies, specialist-supervised immunotherapy may reduce sensitivity to a specific allergen. The appropriate approach depends on the allergy and the individual patient."}]}]}]]}]}]
